  "methodology": {
    "baseline": "Read-only snapshot of built #55 dist before #56/#57; no source or build changes.",
    "fixture": "Public WebGL2Renderer export with static 1024-command, 15-float sprite buffer using placeholder texture, 128x128 CSS/backing pixels at DPR1. No Rust simulation or asset IO in timed regions.",
    "newContext": "Each variant/trial gets a new Chromium browser process and temporary profile, then a new WebGL2 context inside constructor. Disk shader cache disabled. System/driver/compiler caches not purged, so not a guaranteed cold hardware cache.",
    "reusedContext": "Renderer.destroy then new WebGL2Renderer on the same canvas and WebGL context; GPU resources recreated with process/driver shader caches potentially warm.",
    "timing": "performance.now CPU elapsed around constructor/render/renderCommands/renderPostProcess. gl.finish after constructor and each complete frame recorded separately as CPU API-call wall time. Then a synchronous one-pixel readPixels is timed separately to include any pending first-use/queued work plus readback/driver/IPC overhead. Neither finish nor readback timing is pure GPU duration. No GPU timer-query measurements.",
    "warmFrames": "10 requestAnimationFrame-separated frames per renderer after first frame. gl.finish plus synchronous readPixels after each frame. Timing excludes RAF wait.",
    "limitations": [
      "Only this headless Chromium/driver machine at DPR1 and 128x128; not representative hardware/mobile data.",
      "PreserveDrawingBuffer enabled for pixel validation; may differ from production default.",
      "No claim that constructor duration is solely shader compile; contains context/resource creation and resize.",
      "CPU timing quantization and shared-machine scheduling noise apply; reported distributions retain outliers.",
      "Observed SwiftShader readback warm floor ~26-28ms means first-frame synchronous totals must not be presented as production frame time or pure shader compile stalls."
    ]
  },
